# =============================================================================
# Laptop compose credentials — copy to `.env` AT THE REPO ROOT.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# This is NOT `app/.env`. Compose reads the project-root `.env` for `${VAR}`
# substitution in docker-compose.yaml, and nothing else; the app's own config
# stays in `app/.env` (template: `app/.env.example`). A public host uses
# `.env.production.example` instead.
#
# Both values are `${VAR:?}` in docker-compose.yaml, so a missing one fails
# `docker compose up` loudly rather than starting a database with no password.
#
# ⚠️  EXISTING VOLUMES KEEP THEIR FIRST PASSWORD. Neo4j and Postgres apply these
#     only when their data volume is created. If you already have a stack from
#     before this file existed, its volumes were created with `password` — put
#     THAT here (or rotate it in place first). docs/DOCKER_COMPOSE.md,
#     "Credentials and existing volumes", has the exact commands.
#
# ⚠️  THE SAME VALUE LIVES IN MORE THAN ONE PLACE. Compose delivers it to the
#     containers and to the `app` service. It does NOT reach:
#       * configs/mcp-config.yaml — the gateway's `neo4j-cypher.password` and
#         `database-server.database_url` are literals (Compose bind-mounts that
#         file as-is and substitutes nothing into it);
#       * app/.env, for `pnpm dev` on the host — `NEO4J_PASSWORD` and
#         `DATABASE_URL` default to `password` when unset;
#       * the test suites, whose database URLs default to `password` too.
#     Anything but `password` means updating those as well; the doc above
#     lists each one.
#
# Use URL-safe characters (e.g. `openssl rand -hex 24`): the Postgres value is
# spliced verbatim into a `postgresql://` URL, where `/`, `@`, `:` or `#` break
# it. Neo4j 5 rejects passwords shorter than 8 characters.
# =============================================================================

NEO4J_PASSWORD='replace-me-neo4j-password'
POSTGRES_PASSWORD='replace-me-postgres-password'
